---
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import ShoppingList from '~/components/ShoppingList.svelte';
import DeviceData from '~/components/DeviceData.svelte';
import { appendixHref } from '~/data/appendix';
import { allShoppingItems } from '~/lib/data/shopping';

const items = allShoppingItems();
---

<Base
  title="Shopping list"
  nav="shopping"
  description="Every lamp, switch and solenoid marked Fault on this device, grouped by part number."
>
  <h1>Shopping list</h1>
  <p class="muted">
    Parts to order for every component marked Fault on this device, grouped by part number.
  </p>
  <ShoppingList client:load items={items} />
  <section class="kit flush">
    <h2 class="lst-h">Service kit</h2>
    <ul class="lst">
      <li class="lrow static">
        <span class="txt"
          ><strong>Multimeter</strong> with a resistance range that reads below 10 Ω cleanly, for coils
          across the lugs, fuses, and the 5 V, 12 V, 20 V and 50 V test points.</span
        >
      </li>
      <li class="lrow static">
        <span class="txt"
          ><strong>Trifurcon crimp terminals</strong>, .156 inch for the driver board power and lamp
          connectors and .100 inch for the switch and logic connectors, with matching housings and
          square-pin headers, and a ratchet crimp tool made for them. A browned or loose connector
          is replaced, never sprayed.</span
        >
      </li>
      <li class="lrow static">
        <span class="txt"
          ><strong>Contact cleaner</strong>: DeoxIT D5 spray, or the European one-can equivalent
          Kontakt 60 PLUS. Plain Kontakt 60 only with the WL rinse and 61 protectant its maker calls
          for. For intact, merely dirty connectors only.</span
        >
      </li>
      <li class="lrow static">
        <span class="txt"
          ><strong>DeoxIT D100L</strong> or another liquid deoxidizer, for the edge connectors and pin
          headers you can brush.</span
        >
      </li>
      <li class="lrow static">
        <span class="txt"
          ><strong>Isopropyl alcohol</strong> and lint-free wipes, for boards and playfield plastics.</span
        >
      </li>
      <li class="lrow static">
        <span class="txt"
          ><strong>Card stock</strong> for switch contacts: close the switch on it and pull it through.
          No files, no sandpaper, no spray on switches.</span
        >
      </li>
      <li class="lrow static">
        <span class="txt"
          ><strong>Soldering iron</strong> with a fine tip, 60/40 or 63/37 leaded solder, desoldering
          braid, and a header pin extractor for the driver board's IDC connectors.</span
        >
      </li>
      <li class="lrow static">
        <span class="txt"
          ><strong>Spares</strong> that pay for themselves: a set of fuses in every rating on the Fuse
          List, #44 or #47 lamps, #906 flashers, flipper links, a coil sleeve or two, and a 1N4004 diode
          strip.</span
        >
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="gf">
      What the yearly round and the appendices assume is on the bench; the same for every device, so
      it is not tracked here. Why each one, and what not to buy, is in{' '}
      <a href={appendixHref('A7')}>A7 Connectors and cleaning</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
  <DeviceData client:visible />
</Base>

<style>
  .kit {
    margin-top: 24px;
  }
  .kit .lrow {
    align-items: flex-start;
    padding-top: 10px;
    padding-bottom: 10px;
    font: var(--t-sub);
  }
  .kit .txt {
    display: block;
  }
</style>
